```java
BlockingQueue<Task> queue = new ArrayBlockingQueue<>(100);

// producer
void produce(Task t) throws InterruptedException {
    queue.put(t);    // blocks if the queue is full
}

// consumer
void consume() throws InterruptedException {
    Task t = queue.take();   // blocks if the queue is empty
    process(t);
}
```

`ArrayBlockingQueue` handles both wait conditions internally: `put` blocks the calling thread if the queue is full, `take` blocks if it's empty, and both wake correctly the moment space or an item becomes available. No explicit lock, no `wait`/`notify` in your code at all.

```java
// BROKEN: guard is an 'if', vulnerable to spurious wakeup
synchronized Task take() throws InterruptedException {
    if (queue.isEmpty()) {
        wait();      // can return even without a matching notify
    }
    return queue.poll();   // may run on an empty queue!
}

// FIXED: guard is a 'while', re-checks the condition after waking
synchronized Task take() throws InterruptedException {
    while (queue.isEmpty()) {
        wait();
    }
    return queue.poll();
}
```

`wait()` can return without a corresponding `notify()` — a spurious wakeup, permitted by the JVM spec. An `if` guard re-checks the condition zero times after waking, so it proceeds even if the queue is still empty. The fix is always a `while` loop: re-check the condition every time you wake, spurious or not, before proceeding.
